# Elastic Beanstalk options for the mock API.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ONLY NAMESPACES A SINGLE-INSTANCE ENVIRONMENT ACCEPTS
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# This file used to also set `aws:autoscaling:asg` (MinSize/MaxSize) and
# `aws:elasticbeanstalk:environment:process:default` (HealthCheckPath and its thresholds). Both are
# **load-balancer namespaces**: on a single-instance environment EB rejects them and the deploy fails with
# "Your source bundle has issues that caused the deployment to fail" — which names the bundle rather than
# the setting, and sends you looking at the zip.
#
# The one-instance requirement is not lost by dropping them. A single-instance environment *is* one
# instance — there is no Auto Scaling group to pin, which is what the Elastic IP in the create log tells
# you. If this environment is ever recreated as load-balanced, the pin has to come back; see
# ecosystem.config.js for why more than one process is a correctness bug rather than a cost decision.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# S3_BUCKET IS DELIBERATELY NOT SET HERE
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# It was, and it broke the first deploy. The server reads and validates every dataset's document *above*
# `server.listen`, so with a bucket configured and no credentials on the instance the boot read gets a 403,
# the server refuses to start — correctly, and with a clear message — and EB reports only
# "Engine execution has encountered an error". The real reason sits in eb-engine.log where nobody looks
# first.
#
# So the default in *this* file is the bundled documents, which always boot. The bucket and the
# credentials that make it readable are set together, in 02-credentials.config - one file, so the
# failure above is unreachable: a bundle either carries both or neither. That file is gitignored and
# not in .ebignore, so the values are hardcoded into the zip without the key reaching GitHub.
#
# Absent it, this is still the manual equivalent:
#
#     eb setenv AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID=... AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=... S3_BUCKET=contextweave.com
#
# `GET /health` reports `store` so there is never any doubt which one it read: "s3" is the bucket, "file"
# is the copy frozen into the bundle at deploy time.
option_settings:
  # Enhanced health checks this path instead of "/", which the dispatcher answers with a 404 telling a
  # developer the server may be stale — which a checker reads as a failed application.
  aws:elasticbeanstalk:application:
    Application Healthcheck URL: /health

  aws:elasticbeanstalk:application:environment:
    AWS_REGION: us-east-1
    NODE_ENV: production
